# What does NOT go into the @baizor/pipeline tarball.
#
# Before this file existed npm fell back to .gitignore (three lines:
# node_modules/, dist/, *.log) and the published package carried 309 files /
# 5.9 MB unpacked — of which 150 were `*.test.ts` and 2 were CI workflow YAML.
# Every consumer of `bun add -g @baizor/pipeline` was downloading this repo's
# entire test suite.
#
# ── WHY A DENYLIST AND NOT A `files` ALLOWLIST ─────────────────────────────
#
# `bin` is `{"pipeline": "src/cli.ts"}` — RAW TYPESCRIPT. There is no build,
# no bundle, no dist/ in the published artifact (release.yml says so out loud:
# "No build step: this package's bin ships straight from src"). Bun resolves
# the import graph AT RUNTIME, from whatever files the install put on disk.
#
# That inverts the usual packaging risk. With a bundle, a missing source file
# is caught at build time. Here, a module that fails to ship is invisible
# until a user runs the command that imports it — the package installs
# cleanly and crashes on first use. A `files` allowlist is the shape that
# makes that mistake easy: add one module under a directory nobody listed and
# it silently stops shipping. A denylist fails the other way — the worst case
# is shipping a few bytes too many. That asymmetry is the whole argument, so
# do NOT "tighten" this into a package.json `files` field.
# (tests/packed-artifact.test.ts already asserts the templates against this
# reasoning: "The package declares no `files` allowlist, so they ship by
# default".)
#
# ── ⚠ THIS FILE SHADOWS .gitignore ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The moment .npmignore exists npm STOPS consulting .gitignore entirely. The
# three .gitignore entries are therefore repeated verbatim below — delete them
# here and build output starts shipping again.
#
# ── THREE ENTRY POINTS, NOT ONE ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   bin      → src/cli.ts
#   exports  → "."      → src/index.ts
#              "./plan" → src/lib/plan.ts
#
# An import-graph walk from all three (following `from`, `import()`,
# `require()`, bare side-effect imports and type-only imports) reaches 107 of
# the 109 files under src/. The other two — src/lib/hook-runner.ts and
# src/lib/provider-key.ts — are NOT dead:
#
#   * hook-runner.ts is spawned BY PATH, never imported:
#       src/lib/hooks.ts:  join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)),
#                               'hook-runner.ts')
#     No import-graph tool can see that edge. It is the exact hazard that
#     nearly broke the sibling pipeline-runner package's tarball.
#   * provider-key.ts is the single owning module for the provider API key,
#     deliberately kept at arm's length from the rest of src/
#     (tests/output-scrubber.test.ts asserts no sibling imports it).
#
# Which is why NOTHING under src/ is excluded here, by name or by glob — not
# even a would-be `src/**/*.test.ts` rule (there are no colocated tests today;
# a rule for them would be a trap waiting for a file that path-resolution
# depends on to be named unluckily).
#
# ── templates/ MUST SHIP ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# src/lib/templates.ts resolves them against ITS OWN location, never the cwd:
#
#     export const TEMPLATES_DIR = join(import.meta.dir, '..', '..', 'templates')
#
# so `pipeline init` / `pipeline clone` read them out of the installed
# package. A tarball without templates/ works perfectly from a checkout and
# fails on every fresh install — which is why release.yml's "Pack and inspect
# the real tarball" step fails the release when templates/ is missing or
# empty, and why packed-artifact.test.ts asserts example-minimal AND
# support-answer (PIPELINE.md, steps/, scripts/, sample-docs/) are present.
#
# ⚠ EVERY RULE BELOW IS ROOT-ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH. These patterns use
# .gitignore matching semantics, where an unanchored `tests/` or `scripts/`
# matches at ANY depth — and templates/support-answer ships BOTH a scripts/
# directory and a scripts/tests/bm25_retrieve.test.ts inside it, which the
# turnkey `clone support-answer` run needs. An unanchored rule would quietly
# gut the template while every root-level check stayed green.

# ── Carried over from .gitignore (see the shadowing note above) ────────────
node_modules/
dist/
*.log

# ── CI/release automation: consumed by GitHub, never by a consumer ─────────
/.github/

# ── The test suite ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 161 files, ~4.5 MB unpacked, the bulk of the old tarball. Nothing under
# src/ imports anything under tests/ (verified by the graph walk) and no
# shipped file references one by path. They run from a checkout, in CI.
/tests/

# ── The repo's own parallel test runner ────────────────────────────────────
# scripts/parallel-tests.ts exists solely for `bun run test`, which drives
# /tests/ — gone from the tarball with it. (Root-anchored: templates/*/scripts
# is user-facing template content and stays.)
/scripts/

# ── The dev lockfile ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# bun.lock pins THIS repo's dev install. A consumer resolves dependencies
# through their own lockfile and never reads this one; publishing it only
# invites confusion about which tree it describes.
/bun.lock

# ── DELIBERATELY KEPT (do not add rules for these) ─────────────────────────
#
# tsconfig.json — 352 bytes, and load-bearing in a raw-TS package for a
#   reason that is easy to get backwards. It declares no `paths` and no
#   `baseUrl`, so it is not needed to RESOLVE our imports; it is here so Bun,
#   which reads the nearest tsconfig when it executes TypeScript, anchors on
#   OURS instead of walking up into the host project's config when the
#   package sits in their node_modules.
#
# docs/ — 7 files, 224 KB. Kept because shipped content points INTO it:
#   README.md (which ships) has four relative links to docs/cli.md, and
#   shipped source comments cite docs/provider-key.md (src/lib/provider-key.ts),
#   docs/bare-baseline.md and docs/sdk-delta-measurement.md. Nothing prints a
#   docs/ path to a user's terminal at runtime — checked — but a dead pointer
#   in an installed package is still worse than the bytes, and these are
#   markdown next to the megabytes of tests this file removes.
#
# README.md, LICENSE, package.json — npm ships these regardless.
